<h2>The doctrinal knot</h2>
<p>Section 35 of the Indian Stamp Act, 1899 says an instrument chargeable with duty shall not be "admitted in evidence for any purpose" unless duly stamped, and Section 33 obliges every authority before whom such an instrument is produced to impound it. Section 11(6A) of the Arbitration Act, by contrast, confines the referral court to examining "the existence of an arbitration agreement." Which command prevails when an unstamped contract reaches a Section 11 court? <em>SMS Tea Estates</em> (2011) and <em>Garware Wall Ropes</em> (2019) said the court must stop and impound; <em>N.N. Global (2)</em> (2023), by a 3:2 majority, went further and described the agreement as unenforceable, even non-existent in law, until stamped. Within months, the question stood referred to seven judges.</p>

<h2>Inadmissible is not void</h2>
<p>The bench anchored its answer in a distinction that runs through the law of evidence: validity and admissibility are different inquiries. Non-payment of stamp duty is a fiscal lapse the Stamp Act itself treats as curable — pay the duty and penalty, and the same instrument is admitted "for any purpose". A defect the statute permits to be cured cannot make the underlying bargain void ab initio. As the Court put it, non-stamping or improper stamping does not result in the instrument becoming invalid; it becomes, for the time being, inadmissible.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Referral stage (S. 8 / S. 11)</strong> — court sees a prima facie arbitration agreement and refers; it does not impound or adjudicate stamping.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Tribunal constituted</strong> — competence-competence under Section 16 lets the tribunal rule on all objections to the instrument.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Stamping objection before tribunal</strong> — the tribunal, as an authority receiving the instrument in evidence, impounds it and has duty and penalty paid.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Merits proceed</strong> — the cured instrument is admissible for all purposes; no time lost before a referral court.</div>
</div>

<h2>Separability and competence-competence doing real work</h2>
<p>Two principles carried the judgment. Separability treats the arbitration clause as an agreement independent of the contract that contains it, so objections aimed at the main contract do not automatically disable the clause. Competence-competence, codified in Section 16, gives the tribunal authority to rule on its own jurisdiction, including objections to the existence or validity of the arbitration agreement. Reading Section 11(6A) against that design, the referral court&rsquo;s role is deliberately minimal: a prima facie look at existence, nothing more. Every deeper question — stamping, fraud in the underlying contract, novation — belongs, in the first instance, to the arbitrators.</p>

<h2>What changed in day-to-day practice</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Stamp objections no longer stall Section 11 petitions in the Delhi High Court or Section 8 applications before commercial courts; they are recorded and left to the tribunal.</li>
  <li>Respondents who wish to press the objection should raise it at the first hearing before the tribunal, which will impound and direct payment of duty and penalty under the Stamp Act machinery.</li>
  <li>Claimants should nonetheless stamp instruments properly at execution — duty plus penalty (which can be a multiple of the deficit) is an avoidable cost, and revenue consequences survive even though the arbitration proceeds.</li>
  <li>The ruling protects the arbitration timetable in institutional and ad hoc references alike, and applies equally to agreements invoked in interim relief proceedings.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Reading it with the CORE ruling</h2>
<p>Delivered eleven months apart, the seven-judge stamping decision and the Constitution Bench ruling on unilateral appointments form a pair. One clears fiscal objections out of the referral court; the other cleanses the appointment mechanism itself. Together they mean that in a Delhi commercial dispute today, the path from invocation to a properly constituted, objection-resistant tribunal is shorter than it has ever been — provided the clause is drafted with both rulings in view.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> When resisting a reference on stamping grounds, the realistic objective is no longer to block appointment but to have the tribunal impound early, so that duty and penalty fall where the contract allocates costs. When seeking a reference, annex the instrument as it stands and address stamping candidly — concealment invites adverse costs even where the law is now on the claimant&rsquo;s side.</p>
</div>
